          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. The surrounding asides reinforce that this is a restrained avowal rather than a denial of affection.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"since, I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The immediately attached qualification, “According to my bond,” makes this occurrence an affirmation and invocation of Cordelia’s filial bond and its duties, not merely a report that an obligation exists. Her next turn specifies that she returns the duties owed to the father who begot, raised, and loved her. This renews or affirms an existing relational undertaking rather than creating a new promise, and it expressly limits that undertaking rather than pledging unlimited or exclusive love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"You have begot me, bred me, lov’d me: I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The expression is a measured answer defining the extent of Cordelia’s love and duty. Her difficulty putting her heart into words indicates restraint, but the supplied language does not show a reflex-like or affectively discharged response beyond the avowal and affirmation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth:\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and affirming filial obligation, the occurrence functions as a principled limitation and implicit refusal of Lear’s demand for competitive, all-encompassing declarations. Cordelia distinguishes the love due to a father from the love, care, and duty that marriage would properly assign to a husband.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Half my love with him, half my care and duty:\"}]} },\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to state how much they love him while allocating portions of his kingdom according to their answers. After her sisters give expansive declarations, Cordelia answers Lear directly with a restrained avowal qualified by her filial bond and by the limits of the duties proper to that bond.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia affirms that she is bound to Lear by fitting filial duties—obedience, love, and honour—in return for his begetting, raising, and loving her. This is an invocation and affirmation of an existing bond, not a new promise of future conduct or exclusive devotion. Its terms remain general but are partly specified in her following turn. Lear does not accept the bounded formulation as satisfactory; he asks her to “Mend your speech a little,” so the undertaking is expressed but receives adverse uptake.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognize the passage as the opening love-test scene of William Shakespeare’s The Tragedy of King Lear; the supplied source also explicitly identifies the work and author.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene strongly supports the avowal, the invocation of filial obligation, and Cordelia’s resistance to exaggerated or exclusive claims. It does not independently establish every practical term of the filial bond or conclusively prove inner sincerity, although Cordelia’s supplied asides provide affirmative evidence of genuine affection.\"}"
        }
